<title>CAMEROON: Cameroon to increase taxes on agricultural exports.</title>
<headline>Cameroon to increase taxes on agricultural exports.</headline>
<dateline>YAOUNDE 1997-05-01</dateline>
<text>
<p>The Cameroon government is raising the export tax payable on a series of agricultural products from July 1, according to a presidential decree.</p>
<p>On cocoa, cotton, sugar, rubber and medicinal plants, the tax will rise to 15 percent of the FOB value from 13.5 percent.</p>
<p>On coffee, it is rising to 25 percent of the FOB value and on palmoil to 30 percent, in both cases also from 13.5 percent.</p>
<p>However, the decree, dated April 21, said the levy on fresh bananas was being cut to 4,000 CFA francs per tonne from 6,500.</p>
<p>These export taxes were first levied in 1994 after the devaluation of the CFA franc, with the government taking advantage of competitive gains realised by agricultural groups as a result of the parity change.</p>
<p>($=580 CFA francs)</p>
</text>
